1
00:00:00,238 --> 00:00:09,956
You know, for years, the general assumption in the tech world has been that Artificial General Intelligence, or AGI, is going to look like a single, massive, all-knowing supermodel.

2
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:13,460
Just one giant digital brain doing absolutely everything.

3
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:15,740
But what if that's totally backward?

4
00:00:16,340 --> 00:00:23,680
What if the real path to AGI isn't one monolithic model at all, but rather a perfectly orchestrated ecosystem of specialized experts?

5
00:00:24,302 --> 00:00:25,300
Welcome to this explainer.

6
00:00:25,774 --> 00:00:35,060
Today, we're diving into a genuinely fascinating paradigm shift based on a groundbreaking paper called Route Anything, a study of model and policy routing for general-purpose AI systems.

7
00:00:36,046 --> 00:00:40,800
And right off the bat, I want to emphasize that this isn't just some abstract speculative theory.

8
00:00:41,486 --> 00:00:43,780
This research is backed by some serious empirical weight.

9
00:00:44,302 --> 00:00:56,900
We're talking about a massive collaborative effort from brilliant minds at places like the National University of Singapore, the HPC AI Lab, Infraec, Cardinal AI Lab, UC Berkeley, and the Hong Kong University of Science and Technology.

10
00:00:57,518 --> 00:01:05,764
They've essentially cracked open the data on how an AI system can dynamically pick and choose the absolute best model for every single task you throw at it.

11
00:01:06,702 --> 00:01:09,100
So here is our roadmap for today's explainer.

12
00:01:09,774 --> 00:01:10,440
First, 1.

13
00:01:10,798 --> 00:01:12,880
Introduction to AI Routing 2.

14
00:01:13,102 --> 00:01:14,940
A Router's Value 3.

15
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,000
The Potential Gain 4.

16
00:01:17,326 --> 00:01:19,340
Testing Learned Routers 5.

17
00:01:19,598 --> 00:01:22,140
Efficiency and Cheaper Routes 6.

18
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:25,540
3 Rules for Routing Let's jump right in.

19
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:26,580
Section 1.

20
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:34,120
Introduction to AI Routing To really wrap our heads around this, we needed to find two core terms the authors used to set up their whole framework.

21
00:01:34,606 --> 00:01:35,660
It's actually pretty straightforward.

22
00:01:36,366 --> 00:01:37,460
First up, the candidate.

23
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,080
Think of a candidate as just a specific tool in your AI toolbox.

24
00:01:41,742 --> 00:01:46,400
It could be Claude Opus for writing text, or a specific control policy for a robotic arm.

25
00:01:47,182 --> 00:01:48,400
Second, we have the state.

26
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:52,160
The state is basically whatever the router gets to look at before making its choice.

27
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:59,040
It could be the text prompt you just typed in, the general type of task, or even the physical visual scene sitting right in front of a robot.

28
00:01:59,822 --> 00:02:08,700
So the router's entire job is to look at that state and immediately pull the best candidate from the toolbox and to figure out if this orchestration idea actually works.

29
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:11,460
The researchers didn't just test a couple of chatbots.

30
00:02:11,822 --> 00:02:17,260
No, they went huge They built this absolutely massive Omnimodal Trajectory Matrix.

31
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:22,300
To put this scale into perspective, we are looking at 25 incredibly diverse benchmarks.

32
00:02:22,766 --> 00:02:29,500
They analyzed over 374,000 scored runs, testing 452 different candidate configurations.

33
00:02:30,222 --> 00:02:32,040
And this spans across basically everything.

34
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:38,020
Text, image generation, video generation, speech, coding, and even physical robot manipulation.

35
00:02:38,734 --> 00:02:49,100
They basically put every candidate through the exact same paces to see who really reigns supreme on a A Router's Value So this brings up an obvious question.

36
00:02:49,582 --> 00:02:52,660
How do we actually prove an AI router is doing a good job?

37
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:55,800
Well, it all comes down to a really elegant core equation.

38
00:02:56,540 --> 00:03:03,120
A router's true value is simply finding the highest expected quality for a specific input, minus the cost of actually running it.

39
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:04,560
Think of it like this.

40
00:03:05,102 --> 00:03:11,960
If your router is smart, it should match the performance of your absolute strongest, most outrageously expensive model, but at a fraction of the cost.

41
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:19,460
Or, even better, it beats that giant model entirely by routing every unique problem to the exact niche specialist that handles it best.

42
00:03:20,430 --> 00:03:23,540
The Potential Gain Let's look at the hard numbers.

43
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:30,680
How much performance are we literally just leaving on the table if we refuse to route and stubbornly stick to a single model?

44
00:03:31,566 --> 00:03:41,260
Across nearly 20,000 test inputs, if you just default to the single best fixed candidate for a benchmark, you get an average score of about 82.60.

45
00:03:41,870 --> 00:03:42,600
Not bad, right?

46
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:54,800
But if you use what they call a per-input oracle, which is basically a theoretical magical router with perfect hindsight that always picks the right candidate, the score leaps up to 87.98.

47
00:03:55,534 --> 00:03:58,520
That is a massive 5.4 point jump across the board.

48
00:03:59,214 --> 00:04:08,780
It essentially proves that relying on a best single model strategy is just fundamentally flawed, because the best model constantly changes depending on the specific input.

49
00:04:09,742 --> 00:04:14,220
And in some of these domains, that performance gap isn't just 5 points, it is wild.

50
00:04:14,766 --> 00:04:16,300
Look at video generation tasks.

51
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:21,880
The perfect router absolutely crushed the best single fixed model by 18.9 points.

52
00:04:22,478 --> 00:04:26,020
And in robot manipulation, it was an 18.4 point difference.

53
00:04:26,670 --> 00:04:36,500
This clearly tells us there is a gigantic opportunity here for orchestration, especially when we get into highly complex multimodal tasks where no single model can do it all perfectly.

54
00:04:37,454 --> 00:04:42,980
Testing Learned Routers Okay, so an oracle is a nice theoretical concept.

55
00:04:43,470 --> 00:04:48,000
But what happens when we train an actual, practical router to make these choices on the fly?

56
00:04:48,654 --> 00:04:53,040
To really push the limits, let's step out of the digital world and look at embodied AI.

57
00:04:53,614 --> 00:04:57,600
Can't a router literally look at a messy physical scene and make a mechanical decision?

58
00:04:58,478 --> 00:05:00,860
The researchers used RoboTwin data for this.

59
00:05:01,422 --> 00:05:09,120
They fed the router three camera frames and the current state of a robot, and then basically said okay, pick the best control policy for this specific situation.

60
00:05:10,318 --> 00:05:11,860
The results here were phenomenal.

61
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:14,920
The router totally learned how to read the room, so to speak.

62
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:21,400
It figured out that clean, simple scenes needed one policy, while randomized, messy scenes needed a completely different one.

63
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:28,060
By dynamically swapping between them, the router actually beat the success rate of the single best policy by 1.1 points.

64
00:05:28,718 --> 00:05:30,040
But here's the real kicker.

65
00:05:30,414 --> 00:05:33,780
It did that while using 24% less policy inference time.

66
00:05:34,318 --> 00:05:37,920
So it's giving you the best possible outcome, but saving you a ton of time.

67
00:05:38,254 --> 00:05:39,620
Just by adapting to its environment.

68
00:05:40,622 --> 00:05:45,240
Now you might be thinking, great, let's just train routers to pick the winning model every time.

69
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:48,660
But the authors uncovered a massive data trap here.

70
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,900
When training routers, developers naturally try to train them to predict the winner, you know, the model that got the highest score.

71
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:56,380
But check this out.

72
00:05:56,782 --> 00:06:11,340
In the robot training data, 94% of those winner labels were actually just decided by a latency tiebreak, meaning multiple policies successfully completed the task, but one just happened to finish a tiny fraction of a second faster, so it got labeled the winner.

73
00:06:12,206 --> 00:06:15,220
So what happens if you train your router to just find the winner?

74
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:17,100
Well, it gets lazy.

75
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:23,220
It just learns to pick the fastest policy every single time, which completely crashes and burns on complex tasks.

76
00:06:23,726 --> 00:06:27,620
A router trained this way got a pretty dismal 42.8% success rate.

77
00:06:28,174 --> 00:06:40,260
But if you take a different approach, a technique called outcome regression, where you train the router to independently predict the success probability of each policy, bam, the success rate shoots up to 71.6%.

78
00:06:40,270 --> 00:06:41,440
The big takeaway?

79
00:06:42,100 --> 00:06:44,360
Train your router to predict individual outcomes.

80
00:06:44,860 --> 00:06:46,300
Don't just ask it to find a single winner.

81
00:06:47,150 --> 00:06:48,680
Efficiency and cheaper routes.

82
00:06:49,518 --> 00:06:54,100
If you're building systems for an enterprise, this is where the ROI conversation gets really exciting.

83
00:06:54,574 --> 00:07:01,920
Because when you have a pool of candidates that are pretty close in quality but miles apart in cost, a router is basically a financial superpower.

84
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:11,540
Compared to just calling the biggest, most expensive reference model every single time, routing boosts dollar efficiency by an average of 8.82 times across four major testbeds.

85
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:14,280
And it does this with virtually zero loss in quality.

86
00:07:15,630 --> 00:07:20,060
And if you are working specifically with coding tasks, the numbers are astronomical.

87
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:25,440
On the CodeRouter bench, the researchers hit a 25.5 times dollar efficiency gain.

88
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:26,920
Over 25 times.

89
00:07:27,630 --> 00:07:37,600
The router got so incredibly good at matching cheaper, smaller models to the easier coding problems that when configured for zero quality loss, it literally never once had to call the most expensive reference model.

90
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:40,140
It just handled everything perfectly with the cheaper tools.

91
00:07:41,070 --> 00:07:44,360
And as we can see here, this isn't just a fluke in one area.

92
00:07:44,878 --> 00:07:48,380
Finding a cheaper, perfectly capable route is incredibly consistent.

93
00:07:48,960 --> 00:08:01,700
You've got that massive 25.5x gain in code, a 7.1x jump in text question answering, a 1.35x dollar savings in image generation, and a 1.33x time savings in physical robotics.

94
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:07,340
Across the board, routing gives you those high-quality results while burning through way fewer resources.

95
00:08:08,366 --> 00:08:19,220
Finally, Section 6 – Three Rules for Routing To wrap up all this science into something developers can actually use today, the authors distilled their findings down to three golden rules.

96
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:23,760
Rule number 1 – Compare candidates on the exact same inputs.

97
00:08:24,340 --> 00:08:30,300
If a model is missing data, it completely skews your baseline, so you need perfectly matched apples-to-apples comparisons.

98
00:08:31,118 --> 00:08:38,660
Rule number 2 – As we discussed with that latency trap, always train routers on per-candidate outcomes, not just who won.

99
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:45,300
And rule number three, fix your reference model and set your acceptable quality tolerance before you start ranking routes.

100
00:08:45,806 --> 00:08:50,280
This lets you mathematically guarantee your cost savings without accidentally trashing your system's quality.

101
00:08:51,086 --> 00:08:54,580
All of this leaves us with one incredibly provocative question to chew on.

102
00:08:55,246 --> 00:09:01,640
We've clearly seen today that relying on one giant monolithic model just leaves massive performance and money on the table.

103
00:09:01,710 --> 00:09:04,720
The future really seems to belong to the orchestrator.

104
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:16,560
If true AGI requires this diverse, perfectly tuned ecosystem of different candidates, then the most powerful player in AI might not actually be the one who builds the biggest digital brain.

105
00:09:17,198 --> 00:09:19,240
It might just be the one who builds the ultimate router.

106
00:09:20,142 --> 00:09:22,920
Thank you so much for joining me for this explainer, and keep exploring!

